---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="Social Security Glossary - Key Terms Explained | SocialSecurityTiming.com" description="A comprehensive glossary of Social Security terms including FRA, PIA, COLA, spousal benefits, and more. Understand your retirement benefits.">
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    "itemListElement": [
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://socialsecuritytiming.com/" },
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Glossary", "item": "https://socialsecuritytiming.com/glossary/" }
    ]
  })} />

  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
    <nav class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-6">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-primary">Home</a>
      <span class="mx-2">/</span>
      <span class="text-gray-800">Glossary</span>
    </nav>

    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Social Security Glossary</h1>
    <p class="text-gray-600 mb-8">A reference guide to the key terms and concepts related to Social Security retirement benefits. Understanding these terms will help you make a more informed claiming decision. Terms are listed in alphabetical order.</p>

    <dl class="space-y-6">
      <div>
        <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">AIME (Average Indexed Monthly Earnings)</dt>
        <dd class="text-gray-700 mt-1 leading-relaxed">AIME is the average of your highest 35 years of indexed earnings, divided by the total number of months in those years. The Social Security Administration adjusts your past earnings for wage inflation before selecting the top 35 years. AIME is the foundation for calculating your Primary Insurance Amount (PIA). If you worked fewer than 35 years, zeros are included for the missing years, which lowers your average.</dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Bend Points</dt>
        <dd class="text-gray-700 mt-1 leading-relaxed">Bend points are the dollar amounts that define the brackets in the PIA formula. The SSA replaces 90% of AIME up to the first bend point, 32% between the first and second bend points, and 15% above the second bend point. Bend points are adjusted annually based on changes in the national average wage index. They determine how progressive the benefit formula is.</dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Break-Even Age</dt>
        <dd class="text-gray-700 mt-1 leading-relaxed">The break-even age is the age at which the cumulative benefits from delaying Social Security equal the cumulative benefits you would have received by claiming earlier. Before the break-even age, the early filer has collected more in total. After the break-even age, the person who delayed comes out ahead. This concept is central to the claiming age decision, though it does not account for investment returns or taxes.</dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">COLA (Cost-of-Living Adjustment)</dt>
        <dd class="text-gray-700 mt-1 leading-relaxed">COLA is the annual increase applied to Social Security benefits to keep pace with inflation. The adjustment is based on the Consumer Price Index for Urban Wage Earners and Clerical Workers (CPI-W). COLA is announced each October and takes effect in January of the following year. In years with low or no inflation, the COLA may be zero.</dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Delayed Retirement Credits</dt>
        <dd class="text-gray-700 mt-1 leading-relaxed">Delayed retirement credits are the permanent increases applied to your monthly benefit for each month you delay claiming Social Security beyond your Full Retirement Age, up to age 70. For people born in 1943 or later, the credit is 8% per year (or 2/3 of 1% per month). These credits can increase your benefit by up to 24% or 32% depending on your FRA.</dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Early Filing Reduction</dt>
        <dd class="text-gray-700 mt-1 leading-relaxed">If you claim Social Security before your Full Retirement Age, your monthly benefit is permanently reduced. The reduction is 5/9 of 1% per month for the first 36 months before FRA, and 5/12 of 1% per month for any additional months beyond 36. Claiming at 62 (the earliest possible age) can reduce your benefit by 25% to 30% depending on your FRA.</dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Earnings Test</dt>
        <dd class="text-gray-700 mt-1 leading-relaxed">The earnings test applies if you collect Social Security before your Full Retirement Age while still working. If your earnings exceed the annual exempt amount, some of your benefits are temporarily withheld. For every $2 earned above the limit (or $3 in the year you reach FRA), $1 in benefits is withheld. After reaching FRA, there is no earnings test, and your benefit is recalculated to credit back the withheld amounts.</dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Full Retirement Age (FRA)</dt>
        <dd class="text-gray-700 mt-1 leading-relaxed">FRA is the age at which you are entitled to receive your full (unreduced) Social Security retirement benefit based on your earnings record. FRA varies by birth year: it is 66 for those born between 1943 and 1954, and it gradually increases to 67 for those born in 1960 or later. Claiming before FRA results in a permanently reduced benefit, while delaying past FRA earns delayed retirement credits.</dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Government Pension Offset (GPO)</dt>
        <dd class="text-gray-700 mt-1 leading-relaxed">The GPO reduces Social Security spousal or survivor benefits for individuals who also receive a government pension from work not covered by Social Security. The offset reduces your Social Security spousal or survivor benefit by two-thirds of the amount of your government pension. In some cases, the GPO can eliminate the spousal or survivor benefit entirely.</dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Maximum Benefit</dt>
        <dd class="text-gray-700 mt-1 leading-relaxed">The maximum Social Security retirement benefit is the highest monthly amount payable to an individual who has earned at or above the maximum taxable earnings limit for at least 35 years and claims at age 70. This amount changes each year due to COLA adjustments and changes in the maximum taxable earnings. The maximum benefit serves as a useful benchmark for high earners planning their retirement income.</dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Medicare</dt>
        <dd class="text-gray-700 mt-1 leading-relaxed">Medicare is the federal health insurance program primarily for Americans aged 65 and older, though it also covers certain younger individuals with disabilities. Medicare eligibility begins at age 65 regardless of when you claim Social Security. It is important to enroll in Medicare on time to avoid late enrollment penalties, even if you choose to delay your Social Security retirement benefits.</dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Primary Insurance Amount (PIA)</dt>
        <dd class="text-gray-700 mt-1 leading-relaxed">The PIA is the monthly benefit amount you would receive if you claim Social Security at your exact Full Retirement Age. It is calculated by applying the bend point formula to your AIME. The PIA serves as the baseline for all benefit calculations, including early filing reductions, delayed retirement credits, and spousal or survivor benefits.</dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Spousal Benefits</dt>
        <dd class="text-gray-700 mt-1 leading-relaxed">Spousal benefits allow a husband, wife, or qualified ex-spouse to receive up to 50% of the higher-earning spouse's PIA. To qualify, the marriage must have lasted at least 10 years (for ex-spouses), and the claiming spouse must be at least 62 years old. If you claim spousal benefits before your FRA, the benefit is reduced. Spousal benefits do not earn delayed retirement credits beyond FRA.</dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Survivor Benefits</dt>
        <dd class="text-gray-700 mt-1 leading-relaxed">Survivor benefits are paid to the surviving spouse, children, or other qualified dependents when a Social Security beneficiary passes away. A surviving spouse can receive up to 100% of the deceased spouse's benefit amount, depending on the survivor's age at the time of claiming. Survivor benefits can be claimed as early as age 60, or age 50 if the survivor is disabled.</dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Windfall Elimination Provision (WEP)</dt>
        <dd class="text-gray-700 mt-1 leading-relaxed">The WEP is a formula used to reduce Social Security benefits for individuals who also receive a pension from employment not covered by Social Security, such as certain government or foreign pensions. The WEP modifies the standard PIA formula by reducing the 90% factor applied to the first bend point. The reduction is capped, and it does not apply to individuals with 30 or more years of substantial earnings under Social Security.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </div>
</Layout>
